---
/**
 * The playground, one static route per way in.
 *
 * WHAT A ROUTE MEANS NOW. It used to mean "a page about this sample". It means
 * "open the editor with this sample in it". The URLs are unchanged, so every
 * deep link the lessons already emit keeps working, but what a visitor lands on
 * is an editor rather than a listing.
 *
 * `/playground/` is the bare route and it opens on a short starter program
 * rather than on a curated sample, because landing on one of those is exactly
 * the complaint this rebuild answers: a page whose first screen is a
 * hundred-line teaching file is an example page, whatever the button says.
 *
 * WHY A ROUTE PER SAMPLE RATHER THAN A PICKER THAT SWAPS IN THE BROWSER. Each
 * page carries exactly one program and one recorded colouring. The alternative
 * was one page holding all thirteen, which would have meant shipping every
 * listing to every visitor so a control could reveal one of them. A site
 * arguing that a program should link only what it uses does not get to do that,
 * and there is a second reason that matters more: a real URL resolves before
 * any script runs, so a lesson's link works with scripts off and survives being
 * bookmarked or shared.
 *
 * The evidence material that used to live under this page is at
 * `/playground/evidence/`, which is its own file rather than a route generated
 * here, so it cannot collide with a sample id and it does not depend on the
 * recording to exist.
 */
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Playground from "../../components/Playground.astro";
import { compileEndpoint, hasCompileService } from "../../playground/engines/remote";
import type { CuratedSample } from "../../playground/samples";
import { curatedSamples } from "../../playground/samples";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  /* The bare route, plus one per recorded sample. The recording decides what
   * exists, so a sample that failed to record has no page rather than a page
   * that cannot run. */
  return [
    { params: { sample: undefined }, props: { sample: null } },
    ...curatedSamples.map((sample) => ({
      params: { sample: sample.id },
      props: { sample },
    })),
  ];
}

interface Props {
  sample: CuratedSample | null;
}

const { sample } = Astro.props as Props;

const title = sample === null ? "Playground | iyi" : `Playground: ${sample.id} | iyi`;
const summary =
  sample === null
    ? hasCompileService
      ? "An editor you can type iyi into. The source is compiled by a real iyi compiler and the module it produces runs in your own tab against a WASI host written in TypeScript."
      : "An editor you can type iyi into. This build has no compile service, so it runs the recorded samples and says plainly that it cannot compile what you type."
    : `The iyi playground, opened on ${sample.path}. The sample runs from the module the compiler built from it, checked by digest, and the editor takes anything else you type.`;
---

<Page title={title} summary={summary}>
  <Playground sample={sample} />

  <section class="spread">
    <div class="to-wide honest">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <h2>What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        The same treatment the rest of this site gives its own losses, applied to
        this page. Three of these are structural and will not be fixed by
        finishing something.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing in this page compiles iyi.</strong> The compiler
          reports an error by raising, and <code>raise</code> on
          <code>wasm32</code> does not unwind, so a compiler compiled for this
          page could not hand a diagnostic back to whatever called it: it could
          only die. The investigation is at
          <code>doc/website/PLAYGROUND-FEASIBILITY.md</code>. That is why the
          compiling happens elsewhere and only the running happens here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compiling iyi is itself code execution, by design.</strong>
          <code>src/compiler/iyi/macros/methods.cr</code> dispatches
          <code>system</code> and the backtick form through
          <code>interpret_system</code>, which runs a shell command;
          <code>read_file</code> and <code>read_file?</code>, which read any path
          the compiler can reach; <code>env</code>, which reads the compiler's
          own environment; and <code>run</code>, which through
          <code>interpret_run</code> compiles and executes another program and
          substitutes its output into the source being compiled. All of that
          happens at compile time. So posting arbitrary iyi to a compile service
          is remote code execution unless the service contains it, and{" "}
          {
            hasCompileService
              ? "the sentence in the rail above is that service's own account of how it does."
              : "there is no service in this build to contain anything, because there is no service in this build."
          }
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>
            A program that reaches the host at compile time may behave
            differently here than in a local build.
          </strong>
          That follows from the point above rather than from a decision anyone
          made about this page. If the service blocks the macro methods that
          reach the host, then a macro that shells out or reads a file works on
          your machine and does not work here, and the difference is real rather
          than a limitation of the editor. The page will not pretend otherwise,
          and it will not guess which way{" "}
          {hasCompileService ? compileEndpoint : "a service"} resolved it: the
          rail carries the service's own sentence and nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There are no threads, and there cannot be.</strong> GitHub
          Pages cannot set HTTP response headers, so
          <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy</code> and
          <code>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy</code> cannot be sent, so this
          document is never cross-origin isolated, so
          <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> does not exist here. That rules out wasm
          threads and it rules out synchronous standard input through
          <code>Atomics.wait</code>. It is also why output arrives in the chunks
          the program wrote but only once <code>_start</code> returns: a wasm
          call in this page cannot be suspended.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There is no standard input and no filesystem.</strong> Reads
          from descriptor zero succeed and report end of file, which is what a
          program sees when its input is redirected from nothing, and no
          directory is preopened, so every path call fails the way it would under
          a host that granted no capabilities. One curated sample fails on
          purpose for exactly that reason, the recording marks it, and it is in
          the set because a target that cannot open a file is a thing worth
          knowing about the target.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The recordings behind all of this, and the proof that a module printed
        what the native binary printed, are on the
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/*$/, "/")}playground/evidence/`}
          >evidence page</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .honest {
    margin-block: 1rem 3rem;
  }

  .honest h2 {
    margin-block: 1.6rem 0.7rem;
  }

  .honest p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .honest ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.05em;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .honest li {
    margin-block: 0.65em;
  }
</style>
